<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eauze</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elusa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eauze is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elusa">Elusa</a> it was the chief town of the Aquitanian Elusates and in late antiquity the capital of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulana</a> and seat of its metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The Eauze treasure, a hoard of about 28,000 coins and jewellery buried in the 3rd century, was found there in 1985.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eauze.jpg" alt="Eauze" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tournasol7 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Town_hall_of_Eauze_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
